  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern today is defined a trough of \nlow pressure across the Intermountain West with a low centered \nover southern Idaho, a second trough west of this feature aligned\nalong 135W and a strong ridge of high pressure over the SERN \nU.S. with a high center over east Texas. In the lower-to-middle\nlevels of the atmosphere (850 to 700mb), there is an amplified\ntrough of low pressure over the northern Baja Mexico.\n\nThe latest GOES precipitable water imagery indicated dry PWATs \nbetween 0.50 and 0.65 inches across the majority of Southeast \nArizona, with higher values between 0.65 and 0.90 inches NE of \nTucson in Graham/Greenlee counties. There is a thin plume of \ndeeper moisture (PWATs 0.80 to 1.00 inches) observed out ahead \nof the 850-700mb trough axis extending from the Baja Spur into \nthe northern Gulf of California.\n\nAs we move into Sunday, the Intermountain West trough tracks\neast into the Northern Plains, bowling over and suppressing the\nupper ridge across the SERN states. The trough behind it digs\nsoutheast, with a low-center finding its way west of San Francisco\nby 21/00Z Sunday afternoon. This position will force that \nlower-to-mid level feature over the NRN Baja to eject across SW\nArizona tomorrow. Given the weak moisture plume out ahead of \nit, we may have enough lower-level moisture convergence to \nsupport a weak shower to develop across our neck of the woods. I\nwouldn't expect anything strong given the shortwave ridging \noccuring in the middle to upper levels, but since the sub-cloud \nlayer is dry, we may see some gusty/breezy winds to 35 mph \naccompany the light shower downdrafts.\n\nBoth of the upper troughs continue to track east, establishing\nthemselves over the Great Lakes and central California by 22/00Z\nMonday afternoon. Amplified shortwave ridging in between these\ntwo features will support the subtropical ridge to build back\ninto the SERN states the first half of next week. The resulting\nsoutherly flow across Sonora/Chihuahua MX will draw moisture \nnorthward again. Although the bulk of the moisture will advect\ninto New Mexico, we will see a general deepening of moisture \nacross eastern portions of Southeast Arizona, mainly east of \nTucson. At this time, the best deformation between the upper \ntrough to our west and the subtropical ridge to our east will \nset-up across New Mexico. This is supported by the WPC Excessive\nRainfall Outlook (ERO) in the slight category across New Mexico\n(at least 15% risk rainfall will exceed flash flood guidance) \nboth Tuesday and Wednesday. In Southeast Arizona, we should \nhave enough lift/moisture across the eastern half of our CWA to \nsupport scattered showers and thunderstorms. \n\nOtherwise, it is still possible that we will see some tropical \ninfluence the second half of next week into next weekend especially\ngiven the strong ridging to our east. However, plenty of uncertainty\nstill on the timing and track of southerly systems and the overall\nextent of deeper moisture. We will continue the chance of showers\nand thunderstorms with at least the presence of recycled\nmoisture to aid thunderstorm development.",
